1. Concurrency: Concurrency decides whether the ResultSet is read-only or updatable.

CONCUR_READ_ONLY

This is the normal case.

Statement st = con.createStatement(
        ResultSet.TYPE_SCROLL_INSENSITIVE,
        ResultSet.CONCUR_READ_ONLY
);


ResultSet rs = st.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM T1");

Now you can navigate:

rs.next();
rs.previous();
rs.absolute(2);

But you cannot update the ResultSet:

rs.updateString("Name", "Sarthak");  // ❌
------------------------------------------
2. CONCUR_UPDATABLE: This allows you to update/delete the current row through the ResultSet.

Statement st = con.createStatement(
        ResultSet.TYPE_SCROLL_SENSITIVE,
        ResultSet.CONCUR_UPDATABLE
);


ResultSet rs = st.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM T1");

Now:

while (rs.next()) {
    if (rs.getInt("Id") == 101) {
        rs.updateString("Name", "Sarthak");
        rs.updateRow();
    }
}

The database row is updated.

You can also: rs.deleteRow();

while (rs.next()) {

    if (rs.getInt("Id") == 102) {
        rs.deleteRow();
        break;
    }
}
---------------------------------------------------
ResultSet.HOLD_CURSORS_OVER_COMMIT : The ResultSet stays open after commit(), so you can continue reading data from it. ✅

Example:

ResultSet rs = st.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM T1");

rs.next();

con.commit();

rs.next();   // Still possible ✅

Whereas:

ResultSet.CLOSE_CURSORS_AT_COMMIT : The ResultSet is closed after commit(), so you cannot continue using it. ❌